<% content_for :title, "Books | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="perspectives-books-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="perspectives-books-hero">

    <div class="perspectives-books-hero-content">

      <p class="perspectives-books-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / PERSPECTIVES / BOOKS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>SHIT</span>
        <span>WORTH</span>
        <span>READING.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="perspectives-books-intro">
        BOOKS THAT MADE ME THINK.<br>
        BOOKS THAT MADE ME QUESTION SHIT.<br>
        BOOKS THAT ACTUALLY STAYED WITH ME.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- INTRO -->
  <section class="pb-intro-section">

    <p class="pb-number">01 / THE BOOKSHELF</p>

    <h2>
      I DON'T WANT<br>
      A FUCKING<br>
      READING LIST.
    </h2>

    <div class="pb-copy">

      <p>
        This isn't a list of books I'm telling everybody they
        absolutely have to read.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's a collection of books I've read, am reading,
        or keep coming back to because something in them
        made me stop and think.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some are about spirituality. Some are about recovery.
        Some are philosophy. Some are memoirs. Some have absolutely
        nothing to do with sobriety on the surface and still managed
        to teach me something about it.
      </p>

      <div class="pb-callout">
        THE QUESTION ISN'T<br>
        "WAS THIS A GOOD BOOK?"<br><br>
        IT'S "WHAT DID I FUCKING TAKE FROM IT?"
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- BOOKS -->
  <section class="pb-library">

    <div class="pb-library-heading">

      <p>02 / ON MY SHELF</p>

      <h2>
        READ.<br>
        THINK.<br>
        ARGUE WITH IT.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="pb-books-grid">


      <!-- THICH NHAT HANH -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-pink">

        <p class="pb-book-number">01</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          BUDDHISM / MINDFULNESS
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE HEART OF THE<br>
          BUDDHA'S TEACHING
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          THICH NHAT HANH
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            A book I come back to for Buddhism without needing
            Buddhism to become another impossible standard
            I'm supposed to live up to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Suffering, mindfulness, compassion, impermanence,
            craving, and learning how to actually be present
            for your own life.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Suffering is something to understand — not something
            I have to immediately escape.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- DHAMMAPADA -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-yellow">

        <p class="pb-book-number">02</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          BUDDHISM / WISDOM
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE<br>
          DHAMMAPADA
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          BUDDHIST TEXT
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            Short teachings that somehow manage to give me
            plenty of shit to think about.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mind. Anger. Craving. Discipline. Actions.
            Attention. How much suffering we create by grabbing
            onto everything that passes through our heads.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Just because my mind produces a thought doesn't mean
            I have to follow the fucking thing.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- BHAGAVAD GITA -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-purple">

        <p class="pb-book-number">03</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          HINDUISM / PHILOSOPHY
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE<br>
          BHAGAVAD GITA
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          HINDU SCRIPTURE
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            A conversation about duty, action, identity,
            devotion, discipline, and what the hell you're
            supposed to do when you're overwhelmed by the
            situation in front of you.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            I can be responsible for my actions without pretending
            I control every outcome.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- FOUR AGREEMENTS -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-black">

        <p class="pb-book-number">04</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          PERSONAL GROWTH
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE FOUR<br>
          AGREEMENTS
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          DON MIGUEL RUIZ
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            Simple ideas that become significantly less simple
            when you actually try to live them.
          </p>

          <div class="pb-mini-list">
            <span>BE IMPECCABLE WITH YOUR WORD.</span>
            <span>DON'T TAKE THINGS PERSONALLY.</span>
            <span>DON'T MAKE ASSUMPTIONS.</span>
            <span>ALWAYS DO YOUR BEST.</span>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            How much unnecessary suffering comes from deciding
            I know what somebody else is thinking.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- GUCCI MANE -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-yellow">

        <p class="pb-book-number">05</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          MEMOIR / ADDICTION / REINVENTION
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE AUTOBIOGRAPHY<br>
          OF GUCCI MANE
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          GUCCI MANE WITH NEIL MARTINEZ-BELKIN
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            Addiction, jail, consequences, identity, ambition,
            chaos, and rebuilding a life after you've spent a
            long time helping destroy it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every useful recovery book has
            <em>recovery</em> stamped across the cover.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Your past can explain where you came from without
            getting permanent ownership of where you go next.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- BRENÉ BROWN -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-pink">

        <p class="pb-book-number">06</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          SHAME / VULNERABILITY / CONNECTION
        </p>

        <h3>
          BRENÉ<br>
          BROWN
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          VARIOUS WORKS
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            Shame gets really fucking comfortable in secrecy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Her work on vulnerability, shame, courage,
            belonging, and connection gives me another way
            to look at the stories I tell about myself.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Hiding the parts of myself I'm ashamed of doesn't
            automatically make the shame disappear.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- ANNE FRANK -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-purple">

        <p class="pb-book-number">07</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          DIARY / HISTORY / HUMANITY
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE DIARY OF<br>
          ANNE FRANK
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          ANNE FRANK
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            A diary can preserve the inside of a person's life
            when the outside circumstances are trying to reduce
            that person to something else entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters to me as somebody who writes things down
            when life gets fucking weird.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Writing down what happened isn't trivial.
            Sometimes it's how a person refuses to disappear
            inside what happened to them.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- OPEN SLOT -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-empty">

        <p class="pb-book-number">08</p>

        <h3>
          WHAT'S<br>
          NEXT?
        </h3>

        <p>
          The shelf isn't fucking finished.
        </p>

      </article>


    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- HOW I READ -->
  <section class="pb-reading-section">

    <p class="pb-number">03 / HOW I READ</p>

    <h2>
      KEEP WHAT<br>
      MAKES YOU<br>
      THINK.
    </h2>

    <div class="pb-copy">

      <p>
        I don't need to agree with an entire book for one idea
        inside it to matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        I can underline something and disagree with the next page.
        I can learn from a writer without turning that writer
        into an authority over my entire fucking life.
      </p>

      <div class="pb-reading-grid">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>What made me stop reading and think?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>What did I disagree with?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>What connected to something I've lived through?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>What idea followed me after I closed the book?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>Did anything actually change how I behave?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>What do I want to come back to later?</p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="pb-ending">

    <p>
      READ THE BOOK.<br>
      QUESTION THE BOOK.<br>
      WRITE IN THE FUCKING MARGINS.
    </p>

    <h2>
      THINK FOR<br>
      YOURSELF.
    </h2>

    <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
        perspectives_path,
        class: "pb-back-link" %>

  </section>

</main>